From 84f2b1eb87e0f4b899288019a6b6714a4262eaf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:05:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 79/80] drm/pvrsgx: scale the SGX core clock and voltage with devfreq

The OMAP4 vendor kernels scaled the SGX between its OPPs from the DDK's
power hooks: RequestSGXFreq() asked omap_device_scale() for the highest
OPP when work was queued and for the lowest one after an idle timeout,
and omap_device_scale() raised the voltage domain before the clock and
lowered it after. The 1.17 DDK still calls those hooks
(SysSGXCommandPending, SysSGXIdleEntered), but its sgxfreq backend is
not built for OMAP4, so the SGX runs at whatever rate the DT assigned.

Do the same the mainline way. When the gpu node has an
operating-points-v2 table, a "core" clock and a "vdd-core" supply, the
glue (pvr-dvfs.c) registers a devfreq device with simple_ondemand: the
load is the share of time the DDK reported the SGX busy, from a command
queued on an idle SGX to the microkernel's idle event or a power down,
and dev_pm_opp_set_rate() orders voltage and clock. devfreq follows the
device's runtime PM, so a suspended GPU parks at the "opp-suspend" OPP
and resumes at the OPP it left, within the current PM QoS limits
(devfreq alone resumes at the suspend OPP and needs a polling window
to ramp up again, which a GPU sleeping between bursts may never get). With #cooling-cells the device is also
a devfreq cooling device.

The DDK derives its microkernel timer and its APM and recovery timeouts
from the core clock it is told at SGX power-up. Tell it the highest OPP
when scaling (lower OPPs then only lengthen those timeouts, as TI's
sgxfreq does), else the actual rate of the "core" clock, else the
built-in SYS_SGX_CLOCK_SPEED. Without a table, or without
CONFIG_PM_DEVFREQ, nothing changes.

Signed-off-by: Petr Parkan Janda <petronijus@bastla.com>
